<p>A project becomes academically stronger when the student can explain <em>why</em> something works, support a conclusion with evidence, calculate a result, compare alternatives, or revise a design.</p>

<h2 style="color:#d84315;font-size:1.8em;margin-top:40px;">3. Build a Simple Course Around the Work</h2>
<p>Instead of logging “fed chickens — 30 minutes,” build a learning trail:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Question:</strong> What are we trying to understand or solve?</li>
<li><strong>Study:</strong> What books, extension publications, videos, observations, or primary sources did the student use?</li>
<li><strong>Practice:</strong> What did the student actually do?</li>
<li><strong>Measure:</strong> What numbers, observations, samples, or results were collected?</li>
<li><strong>Reflect:</strong> What did the student learn, change, or discover?</li>
<li><strong>Assess:</strong> What evidence shows progress?</li>
</ol>
<p>That structure turns everyday work into a documented academic experience without pretending that every physical chore is automatically a credit.</p>

<div style="background:#e8f5e9;border:2px solid #81c784;border-radius:8px;margin:35px 0;padding:25px;">
<h2 style="color:#1b5e20;margin-top:0;">4. Use Missouri's Free Educational Infrastructure</h2>
<p>You do not need to buy a boxed curriculum for every homestead project. Missouri families can supplement hands-on learning with university Extension publications, youth programs, libraries, museums, government resources, and open educational materials.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>MU Extension:</strong> Gardening, soil, livestock, food systems, beginning farming, and homesteading education.</li>
<li><strong>MU Extension Garden ’n Grow:</strong> Youth gardening resources that naturally support science, measurement, observation, and responsibility.</li>
<li><strong>MU Extension Little Roots, Big Harvest:</strong> Hands-on homesteading education covering gardening, backyard livestock, food preservation, and self-sustaining systems.</li>
<li><strong>USDA Beginning Farmers and Ranchers:</strong> Agriculture education and program information useful for older students exploring farming and rural entrepreneurship.</li>
<li><strong>HWN Academic Framework:</strong> Use the Homeschooling With Necessity framework to map projects, build units, and organize portfolio evidence.</li>
</ul>
