<h2 style="color:#bf360c;margin-top:0;text-align:center;">6. Keep Record-Keeping Simple and Consistent</h2>
<p>A useful daily or weekly log can include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Date</strong></li><li><strong>Activity/project</strong></li><li><strong>Time spent</strong></li><li><strong>Academic connection</strong></li><li><strong>Location</strong></li><li><strong>What was learned</strong></li><li><strong>Evidence saved</strong> (photo, worksheet, graph, journal entry, plan, measurement, etc.)</li>
</ul>
<p>Missouri's statute specifically recognizes a written record of subjects taught and activities engaged in, a portfolio of academic work, and evaluations of academic progress—or equivalent credible evidence. A consistent system makes it much easier to show the story of the student's education.</p>
</div>

<h2 style="color:#d84315;font-size:1.8em;margin-top:40px;">7. Make the Transcript Tell the Story</h2>
<p>When a student completes a substantial project, organize the evidence into a course or project folder. Possible course titles might include <strong>Applied Agricultural Science</strong>, <strong>Homestead Engineering</strong>, <strong>Environmental Science &amp; Land Stewardship</strong>, <strong>Practical Economics</strong>, or <strong>Food Science</strong>—but the title should reflect the actual work and academic content.</p>
<p>Keep the underlying project records. A transcript is the summary; the portfolio is the evidence.</p>

